An applicant can apply to the Court of Appeal for leave to consider a binding precedential decision of the Court. R. v. Melnyck, [2014] A.J. No. 1033, 2014 ABCA 313 at para. 1 (Alta. C.A.). The Court has the ability to overrule precedents, but uses this power cautiously. R. v. Melnyck, [2014] A.J. No. 1033, 2014 ABCA 313 at para. 2 (Alta. C.A.). The court’s function on an application to reconsider is not to determine whether the prior case was wrongly decided, but whether the case should be reconsidered. JL Energy Transportation Inc. v. Alliance Pipeline Limited Partnership, [2024] A.J. No. 575, 2024 ABCA 175 at paras. 5-6 (Alta. C.A.); , [2020] A.J. No. 95, 2020 Edmonton (City) Police Service v. Deluca ABCA 31 at para. 6 (Alta. C.A.); , [2020] A.J. No. 708, 2020 ABCA 251 at para. 5 C. (A.) v. Alberta (Alta. C.A.); , [2021] A.J. No. 868, 2021 ABCA 237 at para. 4 (Alta. C.A.); Peters v. Atchooay Lawrence v. Alberta (Director of SafeRoads), [2023] A.J. No. 994, 2023 ABCA 271 at para. 14 (Alta. C.A.).
The criteria for reconsideration include whether the precedent:
(a) is recent or old;
(b) has been disproved of or is contrary to the decisions of other courts of appeal;
(c) contains some ‘simple, obvious, demonstrable flaw’;
(d) was created by overlooking binding statute or authority;
(e) created settled expectations; or (f) was Reasons for Judgment Reserved or a Memorandum of Judgment.
