Rule 14.47 obligates an appellant whose appeal has been struck, dismissed or deemed abandoned to file, serve, and make returnable a restoration application as soon as is reasonably possible and must be returnable within the time limitations set out in the rule.
Rule 14.47 is written in mandatory terms and must be complied with. Coleman v. Coleman Wass v. , [2014] A.J. No. 1443, 2014 ABCA 452 at para. 21 (Alta. C.A.); Wass, [2020] A.J. No. 521, 2020 ABCA 180 at para. 22 (Alta. C.A.). An appeal which is not restored within this prescribed time period is deemed to be abandoned under rule 14.65(3). If an appeal is abandoned, the party who filed the appeal may not take another step to advance it. Coleman v. Coleman, [2014] A.J. No. 1443, 2014 ABCA 452 at para. 22 (Alta. C.A.); see also Martinez v. Chaffın, [2018] A.J. No. 851, 2018 ABCA 244 at paras. 5-12 (Alta. C.A.). The decision is discretionary, and once an appeal has been deemed abandoned the threshold to restore it is heightened. Rancher Construction Ltd. v. Scott Construction (Alberta) Ltd., [2020] A.J. No. 324, 2020 ABCA An applicant whose dilatory prosecution of an appeal has activated rule 14.65(3) — resulting in their appeal being deemed abandoned — “must present cogent reasons to justify restoration of the appeal” and must meet a rigorous standard. Coleman v. Coleman, [2014] A.J. No. 1443, 2014 ABCA 452 at para. 33 (Alta. C.A.).
